# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **A workflow sequences commands and queries. It spans several transactions, and opens
# none of its own** — each command it calls opens exactly one.
#
# That is a saga, not a unit of work, and the bill comes with it:
#
# - **Every step is idempotent**, because the only recovery from a half-run sequence is to
#   run it again.
# - **Every intermediate state is a legal state.** Stopping at step three of six must leave
#   the world consistent, just less far along.
# - **Undo is another step, not a rollback.** A compensating command is written into the
#   sequence, named and tested like any other, and it is forward motion: you cannot
#   un-send a confirmation, you send a cancellation.
#
# It answers with a Result, and the class method enforces that.
#
# **A workflow does not branch.** Branching is deciding, and a rule has one home. The three
# legal ways to express a condition: stop on `failure`, push the condition into a command
# that no-ops convergently, or choose which workflow to run at the edge.
#
# **A workflow does not call a workflow.** Its whole content is its sequence, and nesting
# hides the only thing it had to offer.
#
# **It names its STEPS, and every permission is checked before the first one runs.** A
# workflow spans several transactions, so a refusal partway cannot undo what came before —
# those transactions closed. Step three refusing leaves steps one and two done, committed
# and visible, with no rollback. Before the first step is the only moment refusing is free.
#
# `STEPS` is the one thing a workflow must declare, because the base class cannot see what
# `call` will do until it does it. `Shipshape/WorkflowAggregatesPermissions` fails the build
# when it disagrees with what the body actually calls, so the list cannot rot in the
# direction that grants.
#
# **There is no default `STEPS`.** An inherited empty list would mean a workflow that forgot
# to declare one checked nothing and ran for everybody — a fail-open reached by omission,
# which is the one thing this model must never allow. Forgetting raises instead.
class Workflow
  include TypedArguments

<%- if auth -%>
  extend Permission

  # Ask a workflow what it needs without running it: hide a button the actor cannot use,
  # populate an admin screen, seed the permission table. Derived from STEPS, so it cannot
  # disagree with what the workflow actually does.
  #
  # **Anonymous steps contribute nothing.** An anonymous operation is never granted — that
  # is what anonymous means — so aggregating its name would demand a grant nobody can hold
  # and make the whole workflow permanently forbidden.
  #
  # `false` refuses the ancestor chain: a subclass declares its own or this raises. An
  # inherited empty list would mean a workflow that forgot to declare one checked nothing
  # and ran for everybody.
  def self.permissions
    raise NotImplementedError, "#{name} must declare STEPS" unless const_defined?(:STEPS, false)

    steps = const_get(:STEPS, false)
    # An empty list is the same fail-open as a missing one: `[].all?` is true, so the
    # workflow would run for an actor holding no grants at all. A workflow with no steps is
    # not a workflow.
    raise NotImplementedError, "#{name} declares no steps" if steps.empty?

    steps.reject(&:anonymous?).map(&:permission).uniq
  end

  # **One predicate, asked twice.** A view asks it to decide whether to offer the button;
  # `call` asks it to refuse. Overriding the inherited singular check matters: that one
  # asks for this workflow's OWN name, which is never granted, so a view using it would
  # hide the button from everybody.
  def self.permits?(actor)
    return true if anonymous?
    raise ArgumentError, "#{name} requires an actor" if actor.nil?

    permissions.all? { |permission| actor.may?(permission) }
  end

<%- end -%>
<%- if auth -%>
  # A workflow every one of whose steps is anonymous is itself anonymous — a signup
  # sequence runs before anyone is identified — and says so the same way an operation does,
  # by implementing `anonymous_call`.
  def self.call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    unless permits?(actor)
      # **The refusal is the entry nobody has when they need it.** Recorded before returning,
      # because a caller who was told no is exactly what somebody comes looking for.
      AuditLog.record(operation: name, outcome: :refused, actor: actor, error: :forbidden,
                      arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded_from_audit)
      return Result.failure(:forbidden)
    end

    operation = new(**arguments)
    result = operation.__perform__(actor)
<%- else -%>
  def self.call(**arguments)
    result = new(**arguments).__perform__
<%- end -%>

<%- if auth -%>
    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with a Result, got #{result.class}" unless result.is_a?(Result)

    # **After the steps, before answering.** A workflow opens no transaction of its own — the
    # steps did — so what this must not sit inside is a step, where a rollback there would
    # erase the entry describing the sequence.
    AuditLog.record(
      operation: name,
      outcome: result.success? ? :succeeded : :failed,
      actor: actor,
      error: result.error,
      arguments: arguments,
      excluded: excluded_from_audit,
    )

    result
  end
<%- else -%>
    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with a Result, got #{result.class}" unless result.is_a?(Result)

    # **After the steps, before answering.** A workflow opens no transaction of its own — the
    # steps did — so what this must not sit inside is a step, where a rollback there would
    # erase the entry describing the sequence.
    AuditLog.record(operation: name, outcome: result.success? ? :succeeded : :failed,
                    error: result.error, arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded_from_audit)

    result
  end
<%- end -%>

  private
<%- if auth -%>

  # Whoever the door was opened for. Most operations never read it, which is why it is here
  # rather than in every initializer.
  attr_reader :actor

<%- end -%>

  def success(value = nil)
    Result.success(value)
  end

  def failure(error)
    Result.failure(error)
  end

  # **Dispatches with an implicit receiver**, so it works whether the operation left its
  # entry point public or made it private. `send` would work too and is banned: it steps
  # over `private`, routes around the call graph, and names a method nobody can grep for. A
  # base class that writes it cannot ask an application not to.
  #
  # Public, and unreachable all the same: `new` is private, so nobody outside can hold an
  # operation to call this on — and `Shipshape/OnlyTheDoorIsCalled` refuses the attempt at
  # the call site, where no visibility keyword can be stepped over.
<%- if auth -%>
  public def __perform__(actor)
    # **The actor belongs to the base class, not to the operation's signature.** The check
    # above needed it; most operations do not, and forcing `actor:` into every initializer
    # would put a keyword in fifty constructors so that three could read it. Set here, and
    # available to any operation that wants it through the private reader below.
    @actor = actor

    self.class.anonymous? ? anonymous_call : call
